Quibble: I define the endgame as starting the moment you kill Rodney
(or, if you purloin the book without killing him, the moment you open
the Sanctum).  That's when the mechanics of the game become materially
different.

>The biggest problems cycle
>among Rodney, spell casters, summon storms, and then riders and
>artifact-wielding player-monsters.
>
>What I call late-game: A boundary somewhere near the Castle and VotD.
>I basically figure the late-game is when you're fully kitted to handle
>Gehenome and maybe you've built up a stash that will be used on the
>way out.  Maybe it starts when you use the Castle wand, maybe when
>you decide you don't need the wishes so you do down below the VotD.
>During the late-game demons are the major enemy.  Summoning demons
>and spellcasting demons put you at risk of dying.

The only scary demons to a character who can credibly survive entering
a place where purple 'L' can appear other than by misfortunate
polymorph are Demogorgon, Orcus, and Dispater.  Orcus is handicapped
by his wand of death, as he'll futilely zap you with his wand
in preference to attacking you. Demogorgon and Dispater aren't
guaranteed to appear, and Demogorgon would be bloody terrifying even
if he didn't cast spells, simply because of the shocking potency of
his sickness attack.

Most demons are harmless to anyone who can handle the Quest; their
melee capabilities are largely neutralised by late-game players'
tremendous defences, and even if you don't have a silver weapon they
die quite quickly in melee combat.  If you have reliable Magic Missile,
they just evaporate; the terrain heavily favours player-launched
beam attacks in Gehennom as long as the player has reflection for
self-protection.

A marilith or balrog who laid hands on a decent artifact would be
scary, but that's about it - and balrogs are incapable of being really
scary due to their pitifully low speed and inability to summon aid.

In the stage you refer to as the "late game", purple 'L' are the major
enemy - and the prudent player obliterates them with a blessed scroll
of genocide, making this stage of the game very safe and hence rather
dull.  Minotaurs (2x 3-30 1x 2-16) and jabberwocks (4x 2-20), of course,
will puree heroes whose AC and HP aren't up to scratch, as will
mariliths who get their hands on decent-quality weapons.

>What I call mid-game: Castle/VotD at the bottom, Medusa, Quest,
>Ludios and such.  Angels appear and are a major obstacle unless
>they are peacefull and you avoid them.

I generally windshield 'A'.
